Question 6: Design Reflection

Currently, the student data is stored in dictionaries where each student has a 
name and a nested dictionary of courses with lists of marks. Separate functions 
like calculate_average(), assign_grade(), and determine_status() operate on this 
dictionary data by receiving it as parameters.

Python allows us to represent one student as a single programming entity by using 
classes. A Student class would contain the student's name and courses as attributes 
(data), and methods like calculate_average(), assign_grade(), and determine_status() 
as behaviors that operate on that student's own data. Each student would be an 
object instantiated from the Student class, encapsulating both the state (name, 
courses, marks) and the operations performed on that state.

This reorganization would make the code more intuitive because instead of passing 
dictionaries around and calling standalone functions, we would simply call 
student.calculate_average() or student.determine_status(). The class would handle 
the logic internally, and each student object would manage its own data. This is 
the core principle of Object-Oriented Programming: bundling data and the methods 
that operate on that data into a single unit called an object.